# 0007. snacks.nvim for UI pieces, oil.nvim for files

- Status: Accepted
- Date: 2026-11-07

snacks.nvim provides most small UI pieces, oil.nvim edits directories as text, and
telescope stays only for find and grep, loaded on first use.

## Technical details
- **Problem**: UI pieces (start screen, notifications, prompts, pickers, buffer delete) came
  from separate plugins or were missing; the telescope file browser made telescope load at
  startup.
- **snacks.nvim** (`lua/plugins/ui.lua`): `lazy = false`, `priority = 1000` (as
  `:checkhealth snacks` asks). Enabled modules:
  - `dashboard`: start screen with the OS logo, wordmark, shortcuts (lazygit only if
    installed), recent files and startup time;
  - `notifier` (compact, 3 s; `\n` shows history), `input` (rename and prompts in a small box
    at the cursor);
  - `words` (highlight other uses of the word; `]]`/`[[` jump between them via LSP);
  - `bufdelete` on `\e` (keeps the window layout), `lazygit` float on `\gg`, file history on
    `\gl`;
  - `bigfile` (huge files open with heavy features off), and `Snacks.picker` for small
    lists such as the ADR picker ([0012](0012-adr-files-in-the-editor.adr)).
- **oil.nvim** (`lua/plugins/editor.lua`): `lazy = false` so `nvim .` opens a directory;
  `-` opens the parent directory; global keys like `<C-p>` stay working inside oil. It
  replaces telescope-file-browser (`\fb` still opens a browser).
- **telescope**: `<C-p>` files, `<C-f>` live grep, `\bb` buffers, `\fh` help, `\fg` grep
  in cwd, `\fr` recent, `\fd` diagnostics, `\fs` symbols, `\fw` word, `\fk` keymaps;
  telescope-fzf-native if `make` exists. Loaded only on these keys.
- **Consequences**: fewer plugins for more features, one look, faster start. snacks and oil
  are among the few `lazy = false` plugins ([0002](0002-lazy-nvim-pinned-and-lazy-loaded.adr)).

## Technologies and alternatives
- Involved: snacks.nvim, oil.nvim, telescope.nvim, telescope-fzf-native, lazygit.
- Alternatives: telescope-file-browser: kept telescope in the startup path.
